    \item Replace $(a,b) \leftarrow (b,r)$.
    \item Repeat until $r = 0$.
    \item $\gcd =$ last non-zero $r$.
    \item For LCM: $\text{lcm} = ab / \gcd$.
\end{enumerate}

\vspace{0.5cm}

The \textbf{Caesar cipher} (or \textit{shift cipher}) with shift $k$ encodes each
letter by shifting it $k$ positions forward in the alphabet:

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Encryption:} $E(p) = (p + k) \bmod 26$

\textbf{Decryption:} $D(c) = (c - k) \bmod 26$

\vspace{0.3cm}
where $p$ is the plaintext letter number ($0$--$25$) and $c$ is the ciphertext
letter number. The key is $k \in \{0,1,\ldots,25\}$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Key Points:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item Julius Caesar used $k = 3$: A$\to$D, B$\to$E, \ldots, Z$\to$C.
    \item Only 26 possible keys --- easily broken by exhaustive search.
    \item Decryption is the inverse: shift back by $k$.
    \item $(c - k) \bmod 26$: add 26 if the result is negative.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textbf{(a)} Encrypt the message \texttt{HELLO} using the Caesar cipher with $k = 3$.

\textbf{(b)} Decrypt the ciphertext \texttt{KHOOR} using $k = 3$.

\textbf{(c)} If the ciphertext is \texttt{PBZR} and the key is $k = 7$,
find the plaintext.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(a) Encrypt \texttt{HELLO}, $k = 3$:}

